\begin{abstract}
We develop, at the level of explicit $2\times2$ matrices, the dictionary between the upper half-plane and the unit disc models of the hyperbolic plane, and use it to characterise exactly which M\"obius transformations preserve the horocycle foliation at the cusp $\infty$.

Three groups of results are established. First, a \emph{horocycle rigidity theorem}: a real matrix $M$ with $\det M = 1$ satisfies $\Imm(M\cdot z) = \Imm z$ for every $z$ in the upper half-plane if and only if $M = \pm T(t)$, where $T(t) = \begin{psmallmatrix}1&t\\0&1\end{psmallmatrix}$; consequently such an $M$ obeys the parabolic trace condition $(\tr M)^2 = 4\det M$. The converse requires the guard that $M$ fix the cusp $\infty$, and we exhibit the matrix $\begin{psmallmatrix}1&0\\1&1\end{psmallmatrix}$---parabolic, determinant one, yet halving the altitude of $i$---to show the guard cannot be dropped. We complete the picture with an explicit conjugacy classification: every non-identity determinant-one real matrix of trace $2$ is $\SL$-conjugate to a nontrivial translation.

Second, a \emph{matrix-level intertwining identity} for the Cayley transform. With $K = \begin{psmallmatrix}i&1\\-i&1\end{psmallmatrix}$, whose M\"obius action is $C(z) = (1+iz)/(1-iz)$, we prove $K\,T(t) = P(t)\,K$ where $P(t)$ is an $\SU$ parabolic fixing the boundary point $-1$. Introducing the disc horocycle function $h(w) = (1-|w|^2)/|w+1|^2$, we prove the exact dictionary $h(C(z)) = \Imm z$ and deduce that $P(t)$ preserves $h$ and the open disc; both reduce to the twin identities $\nu+\delta = w+1$ and $|\delta|^2-|\nu|^2 = 1-|w|^2$.

Third, an \emph{elliptic contrast}: the matrices $S(a) = \begin{psmallmatrix}1&a\\-a&1\end{psmallmatrix}$, implementing $x\oplus y = (x+y)/(1-xy)$, are conjugated by $K$ to diagonal rotations, yielding the linearisation $C(x\oplus y) = C(x)C(y)$ and the discriminant dichotomy separating the parabolic and elliptic families.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} M\"obius action, Cayley transform, horocycle, parabolic subgroup, $\SL$, $\SU$, discriminant trichotomy, velocity addition.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

The upper half-plane $\HH = \{z\in\CC:\Imm z>0\}$ with the metric $ds = |dz|/\Imm z$ is the standard arena for modular forms, Fuchsian groups and hyperbolic dynamics. Its orientation-preserving isometry group is $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\RR)$, acting by fractional linear (M\"obius) transformations. Among these, a distinguished role is played by the isometries preserving the \emph{horocycle foliation at the cusp $\infty$}, i.e.\ the family of horizontal lines $\Imm z = c$; these are the orbits of the horocycle flow.

Folklore asserts that the horocycle-preserving isometries are precisely the horizontal translations, and that these are precisely the parabolic elements fixing $\infty$. Both are correct, but the second needs care: parabolicity is conjugacy-invariant (a condition on the discriminant $(\tr)^2-4\det$), while horocycle preservation \emph{at $\infty$} is not, since it names a specific cusp. This paper isolates where the two conditions agree and exhibits the minimal counterexample where they diverge.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item A self-contained treatment of the M\"obius action of $2\times2$ complex matrices, with the imaginary-part transformation law and the cocycle law stated with explicit non-degeneracy hypotheses (\S\ref{sec:mobius}).
\item The horocycle rigidity theorem and its sharp guarded converse, with the counterexample certifying necessity of the guard (\S\ref{sec:rigidity}).
\item The matrix-level intertwining identity for the Cayley transform, the resulting $\SU$ parabolic one-parameter group, and the exact horocycle dictionary $h\circ C = \Imm$ (\S\ref{sec:cayley}--\S\ref{sec:dictionary}).
\item The elliptic contrast, linearising velocity addition, and the discriminant dichotomy (\S\ref{sec:elliptic}).
\item An explicit conjugacy classification of real parabolics in half-plane and disc form (\S\ref{sec:classification}).
\end{enumerate}

Every statement is proved by finite algebra: $2\times2$ matrix products, evaluation at well-chosen test points, and modulus computations.

\section{The M\"obius action}\label{sec:mobius}

\begin{definition}[M\"obius action]
For $M = \begin{pmatrix}\alpha&\beta\\\gamma&\delta\end{pmatrix}$ with complex entries and $z\in\CC$ with $\gamma z+\delta\neq0$, set
\[
M\cdot z \;=\; \frac{\alpha z+\beta}{\gamma z+\delta}.
\]
For a real matrix $M$ we write $M\cdot z$ for the action of its complexification.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Imaginary-part transformation law]\label{lem:imlaw}
Let $a,b,c,d\in\RR$ and $z\in\CC$ with $cz+d\neq0$. Then
\[
\Imm\!\left(\frac{az+b}{cz+d}\right) \;=\; \frac{(ad-bc)\,\Imm z}{|cz+d|^2}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Use $\Imm(u/v) = (\Imm(u)\Ree(v)-\Ree(u)\Imm(v))/|v|^2$ with $u = az+b$, $v = cz+d$. Since $a,b,c,d$ are real, $\Imm u = a\Imm z$, $\Ree u = a\Ree z+b$, $\Imm v = c\Imm z$, $\Ree v = c\Ree z+d$. Expanding, the terms containing $\Ree z$ cancel and $(ad-bc)\Imm z$ remains.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Non-vanishing denominator]\label{lem:den}
If $M$ is real with $\det M = 1$ and $\Imm z>0$, then $cz+d\neq0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$cz+d=0$ forces $c\Imm z = 0$, hence $c=0$, hence $d=0$, hence $\det M = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Cocycle law]\label{lem:cocycle}
For complex $M,N$ and $z$ with $N_{21}z+N_{22}\neq0$ and the resulting denominators nonzero,
$(MN)\cdot z = M\cdot(N\cdot z)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $D = N_{21}z+N_{22}$. Substituting $N\cdot z = (N_{11}z+N_{12})/D$ into numerator and denominator of $M\cdot(\,\cdot\,)$ and clearing $D$ reproduces exactly the numerator and denominator of $(MN)\cdot z$, whose entries are $M_{i1}N_{1j}+M_{i2}N_{2j}$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:cocycle} is what makes the development \emph{matrix-level}: geometric identities between transformations can be verified as identities between matrices, up to the nonzero scalars that the M\"obius action ignores.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:vert}
For real $c,d,y$: $|c(iy)+d|^2 = d^2+c^2y^2$.
\end{lemma}

\section{Translations as determinant-one matrices}

\begin{definition}
For $t\in\RR$, $\;T(t) = \begin{pmatrix}1&t\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\det T(t) = 1$, $\tr T(t) = 2$, hence $(\tr T(t))^2 = 4 = 4\det T(t)$: $T(t)$ satisfies the parabolic trace condition.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:onepar}
$T(s)T(t) = T(s+t)$, $T(0) = I$, $T(t)^n = T(nt)$ for $n\in\mathbb{N}$, and $t\mapsto T(t)$ is injective. Thus $t\mapsto T(t)$ is a faithful embedding of $(\RR,+)$ into $\SL$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The product law is a direct multiplication; powers follow by induction; injectivity is read off the $(1,2)$ entry.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:transaction}
$T(t)\cdot z = z+t$ for all $z\in\CC$.
\end{proposition}

Thus each horocycle $\Imm z = c$ is mapped to itself by $T(t)$. The next section shows nothing else does.

\section{Horocycle rigidity}\label{sec:rigidity}

Call a real matrix $M$ \emph{horocycle-preserving (at $\infty$)} if $\Imm(M\cdot z)=\Imm z$ for all $z\in\HH$.

\begin{theorem}[Horocycle rigidity]\label{thm:rigidity}
Let $M = \begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}$ be real with $\det M = 1$. Then $M$ is horocycle-preserving if and only if $M = T(t)$ or $M = -T(t)$ for some $t\in\RR$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) For $M = T(t)$ this is Proposition~\ref{prop:transaction}. For $M=-T(t)$ the M\"obius action is unchanged, $(-z-t)/(-1) = z+t$.

($\Rightarrow$) By Lemma~\ref{lem:den} the denominator never vanishes on $\HH$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:imlaw} with $\det M = 1$ gives $\Imm z/|cz+d|^2 = \Imm z$ for all $z\in\HH$; since $\Imm z>0$, this forces the normalisation identity
\[
|cz+d|^2 = 1\qquad (z\in\HH).
\]
Specialising to $z = iy$ and using Lemma~\ref{lem:vert}, $d^2 + c^2y^2 = 1$ for all $y>0$. Two test points suffice: $y=1$ and $y=2$ give $d^2+c^2 = 1$ and $d^2+4c^2 = 1$, so $3c^2 = 0$, i.e.\ $c = 0$, and then $d^2 = 1$. With $c=0$, $\det M = ad = 1$ gives $a = d$. If $d=1$ then $M = T(b)$; if $d=-1$ then $M = -T(-b)$.
\end{proof}

An \emph{a priori} infinite family of constraints collapses to two linear equations in $c^2,d^2$: the rigidity is genuinely two-point rigidity.

\begin{corollary}[Horocycle equation $\Rightarrow$ parabolic trace condition]\label{cor:parab}
If $M$ is real, $\det M = 1$ and $M$ is horocycle-preserving, then $(\tr M)^2 = 4\det M$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity}, $M = \pm T(t)$, so $\tr M = \pm2$ and $\det M = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Guarded converse]\label{thm:guarded}
Let $M$ be real with $\det M = 1$, $(\tr M)^2 = 4\det M$ and $M_{21} = c = 0$. Then $M$ is horocycle-preserving.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From $c=0$, $\det M = ad = 1$; the trace condition $(a+d)^2 = 4$ with $a = 1/d$ gives $(d^2+1)^2 = 4d^2$, i.e.\ $(d^2-1)^2 = 0$, so $d^2 = 1$. Then $|cz+d|^2 = d^2 = 1$ for every $z$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:imlaw} yields $\Imm(M\cdot z) = \Imm z$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The guard is necessary]\label{thm:counter}
Let $N = \begin{pmatrix}1&0\\1&1\end{pmatrix}$. Then $\det N = 1$ and $(\tr N)^2 = 4 = 4\det N$, so $N$ is parabolic; yet $\Imm(N\cdot i) = \tfrac12 \neq 1 = \Imm(i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\det N = 1$, $\tr N = 2$. Here $c=d=1$, so $|c\,i+d|^2 = |1+i|^2 = 2$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:imlaw} gives $\Imm(N\cdot i) = 1/2$.
\end{proof}

Geometrically, $N$ is parabolic about the cusp $0$: $N\cdot z = z/(z+1)$ fixes $0$, and it preserves the horocycles tangent to $\RR$ at the origin. Hence the exact characterisation is the conjunction
\[
\text{horocycle-preserving at }\infty \iff \big[(\tr M)^2 = 4\det M \ \wedge\ M_{21} = 0\big] \iff M = \pm T(t).
\]

\begin{proposition}[Fixed point of a real parabolic]\label{prop:fixed}
Let $M$ be real with $\det M = 1$, $\tr M = 2$ and $c = M_{21}\neq0$. Then $x_0 = (a-d)/(2c)$ is a fixed point of the M\"obius action of $M$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $a+d=2$, $c x_0 + d = (a-d)/2 + d = (a+d)/2 = 1$, so the denominator at $x_0$ is $1$; it remains to check $ax_0+b = x_0$, which after clearing $2c$ reduces, using $bc = ad-1$ and $d = 2-a$, to the identity $a(a-d)+2bc = a-d$.
\end{proof}

So a parabolic with $c\neq0$ has its unique fixed point in $\RR$, and one with $c=0$ has it at $\infty$: in all cases exactly one boundary fixed point.

\section{The Cayley transform at matrix level}\label{sec:cayley}

\begin{definition}
$C(z) = \dfrac{1+iz}{1-iz}$ for $z\neq -i$, and $K = \begin{pmatrix}i&1\\-i&1\end{pmatrix}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\det K = 2i\neq0$ and $K\cdot z = C(z)$. The denominator $1-iz$ vanishes only at $z = -i$, so $C$ is defined on $\overline{\HH}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[$C$ maps $\HH$ into the disc]\label{prop:disc}
If $\Imm z>0$ then $|C(z)|<1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $z = x+iy$, $|1+iz|^2 = (1-y)^2+x^2$ and $|1-iz|^2 = (1+y)^2+x^2$, whose difference is $-4y<0$.
\end{proof}

Also $|C(x)| = 1$ for real $x$, and $C(z)\to -1$ as $z\to\infty$: the boundary $\RR\cup\{\infty\}$ maps onto the unit circle with $\infty\mapsto -1$.

\begin{definition}[Disc-side parabolic]
\[
P(t) \;=\; \begin{pmatrix} 1+\tfrac{it}{2} & \tfrac{it}{2}\\[3pt] -\tfrac{it}{2} & 1-\tfrac{it}{2}\end{pmatrix}, \qquad t\in\RR.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Matrix-level compatibility of the Cayley transform with translations]\label{thm:intertwine}
For every $t\in\RR$, \; $K\,T(t) = P(t)\,K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
On the left,
\[
\begin{pmatrix}i&1\\-i&1\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1&t\\0&1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} i & 1+it\\ -i & 1-it\end{pmatrix}.
\]
On the right, the $(1,1)$ entry is $(1+\tfrac{it}{2})i + \tfrac{it}{2}(-i) = i - \tfrac t2 + \tfrac t2 = i$; the $(1,2)$ entry is $(1+\tfrac{it}{2})+\tfrac{it}{2} = 1+it$; the $(2,1)$ entry is $-\tfrac{it}{2}\,i + (1-\tfrac{it}{2})(-i) = \tfrac t2 - i - \tfrac t2 = -i$; the $(2,2)$ entry is $-\tfrac{it}{2}+1-\tfrac{it}{2} = 1-it$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[M\"obius-level compatibility]\label{cor:mobcomp}
For $z\neq-i$ and $t\in\RR$, \; $C(z+t) = P(t)\cdot C(z)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:cocycle} to both sides of Theorem~\ref{thm:intertwine} acting on $z$: $(K T(t))\cdot z = K\cdot(z+t) = C(z+t)$ and $(P(t)K)\cdot z = P(t)\cdot C(z)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Structure of the disc-side family]\label{prop:discstruct}
For all $s,t\in\RR$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\det P(t) = 1$, $\tr P(t) = 2$, hence $(\tr P(t))^2 = 4\det P(t)$;
\item $P(s)P(t) = P(s+t)$, $P(0) = I$, $P(t)^n = P(nt)$, and $t\mapsto P(t)$ is injective;
\item $\overline{P(t)_{11}} = P(t)_{22}$ and $\overline{P(t)_{12}} = P(t)_{21}$, i.e.\ $P(t)\in\SU$;
\item $P(t)\cdot(-1) = -1$, and for $t\neq0$ this is the unique fixed point.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) $\det P(t) = (1+\tfrac{it}{2})(1-\tfrac{it}{2}) + (\tfrac{it}{2})^2 = 1$. (2) Direct multiplication and induction; injectivity from the $(1,2)$ entry. (3) Conjugation negates $i$ and exchanges the diagonal (resp.\ off-diagonal) entries. (4) At $w = -1$ the numerator is $-(1+\tfrac{it}{2})+\tfrac{it}{2} = -1$ and the denominator is $1$; the fixed-point equation reduces to $\tfrac{it}{2}(w+1)^2 = 0$, a double root at $w=-1$ when $t\neq0$.
\end{proof}

Item (1) is a consistency check: conjugation preserves $(\tr)^2/\det$, so the disc image of a parabolic is parabolic. Item (4) identifies $-1$ as the image of the cusp $\infty$.

\section{The horocycle dictionary}\label{sec:dictionary}

\begin{definition}
For $w\neq-1$, \; $h(w) = \dfrac{1-|w|^2}{|w+1|^2}$.
\end{definition}

The level sets $h = c>0$ are exactly the Euclidean circles inside the unit disc tangent to the unit circle at $-1$; $h = 0$ is the circle minus $-1$; $h<0$ outside.

\begin{theorem}[Horocycle dictionary]\label{thm:dict}
For every $z\neq-i$, \; $h(C(z)) = \Imm z$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $z = x+iy$, $A = |1+iz|^2 = (1-y)^2+x^2$, $B = |1-iz|^2 = (1+y)^2+x^2>0$, so $|C(z)|^2 = A/B$ and
\[
1 - |C(z)|^2 = \frac{B-A}{B} = \frac{4y}{B}.
\]
The key algebraic identity is
\[
C(z)+1 = \frac{1+iz}{1-iz}+1 = \frac{2}{1-iz}, \qquad\text{so}\qquad |C(z)+1|^2 = \frac4B .
\]
Dividing, $h(C(z)) = (4y/B)/(4/B) = y$.
\end{proof}

Fix $t$ and write, for $w\in\CC$,
\[
\nu = \Big(1+\tfrac{it}{2}\Big)w + \tfrac{it}{2}, \qquad \delta = -\tfrac{it}{2}w + \Big(1-\tfrac{it}{2}\Big),
\]
the numerator and denominator of $P(t)\cdot w$. Put $\alpha = 1+\tfrac{it}{2}$, $\beta = \tfrac{it}{2}$, so $\nu = \alpha w+\beta$ and $\delta = \bar\beta w + \bar\alpha$.

\begin{lemma}[Affine-sum identity]\label{lem:sum}
$\nu+\delta = w+1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[$\SU$ pseudo-norm identity]\label{lem:norm}
$|\delta|^2-|\nu|^2 = 1-|w|^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$|\delta|^2 - |\nu|^2 = \big(|\beta|^2|w|^2+|\alpha|^2+2\Ree(\bar\beta\alpha w)\big) - \big(|\alpha|^2|w|^2+|\beta|^2+2\Ree(\alpha\bar\beta w)\big) = (|\alpha|^2-|\beta|^2)(1-|w|^2)$, and $|\alpha|^2-|\beta|^2 = 1+\tfrac{t^2}{4}-\tfrac{t^2}{4} = 1$, which is $\det P(t) = 1$ in $\SU$ form.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Non-degeneracy]\label{lem:nondeg}
If $|w|\le1$ then $\delta\neq0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\delta=0$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:norm} give $-|\nu|^2 = 1-|w|^2\ge0$, so $\nu = 0$ and $|w| = 1$; Lemma~\ref{lem:sum} then gives $w = -1$, but $\delta = 1$ there.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Horocycle invariance on the disc]\label{thm:invariance}
For every $t\in\RR$ and every $w$ with $|w|\le1$, \; $h(P(t)\cdot w) = h(w)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:nondeg}, $P(t)\cdot w = \nu/\delta$ is defined. By Lemma~\ref{lem:norm},
$1-|\nu/\delta|^2 = (|\delta|^2-|\nu|^2)/|\delta|^2 = (1-|w|^2)/|\delta|^2$, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:sum},
$|\nu/\delta+1|^2 = |\nu+\delta|^2/|\delta|^2 = |w+1|^2/|\delta|^2$. Dividing, the factors $|\delta|^{-2}$ cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Disc preservation]
If $|w|<1$ then $|P(t)\cdot w|<1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:norm} gives $|\delta|^2-|\nu|^2 = 1-|w|^2>0$, so $|\nu|<|\delta|$.
\end{proof}

\section{The elliptic contrast}\label{sec:elliptic}

\begin{definition}
For real $x,y$ with $xy\neq1$, \; $x\oplus y = \dfrac{x+y}{1-xy}$, and $S(a) = \begin{pmatrix}1&a\\-a&1\end{pmatrix}$, $R(a) = \begin{pmatrix}1+ia&0\\0&1-ia\end{pmatrix}$.
\end{definition}

The operation $\oplus$ is the tangent addition law, and $S(a)\cdot z = (z+a)/(1-az)$, so $x\oplus a = S(a)\cdot x$. Note $\tr S(a) = 2$ and $\det S(a) = 1+a^2$.

\begin{theorem}[Cayley conjugation of the elliptic family]\label{thm:ell}
For every $a\in\RR$, \; $K\,S(a) = R(a)\,K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$K S(a) = \begin{pmatrix} i-a & 1+ia\\ -i-a & 1-ia\end{pmatrix}$ and $R(a)K = \begin{pmatrix}(1+ia)i & 1+ia\\ -(1-ia)i & 1-ia\end{pmatrix}$; since $(1+ia)i = i-a$ and $-(1-ia)i = -i-a$, the two agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\tr R(a) = \tr S(a) = 2$ and $\det R(a) = (1+ia)(1-ia) = 1+a^2 = \det S(a)$; moreover $R(a)\cdot w = C(a)w$, multiplication by the unimodular number $C(a) = (1+ia)/(1-ia)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Linearisation of velocity addition]\label{thm:lin}
For real $x,y$ with $1-xy\neq0$, \; $C(x\oplus y) = C(x)\,C(y)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(1+ix)(1+iy) = (1-xy)+i(x+y)$ and $(1-ix)(1-iy) = (1-xy)-i(x+y)$, so
\[
C(x)C(y) = \frac{(1-xy)+i(x+y)}{(1-xy)-i(x+y)} = \frac{1+i\frac{x+y}{1-xy}}{1-i\frac{x+y}{1-xy}} = C(x\oplus y). \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For real $a,x$ with $1-xa\neq0$, \; $R(a)\cdot C(x) = C(x\oplus a)$: the elliptic analogue of $P(t)\cdot C(z) = C(z+t)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Boundary parametrisation]\label{prop:bdry}
$C$ is injective on $\RR$; $C(x)\neq-1$ for every real $x$; and if $|w| = 1$ with $w\neq-1$, then $w = C(x)$ for the unique real
$x = \Ree\big(-i(w-1)/(w+1)\big)$. Thus $C:\RR\to S^1\setminus\{-1\}$ is a bijection, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:lin} it carries $\oplus$ to multiplication.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Discriminant dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
For all real $t$ and all real $a\neq0$,
\[
(\tr T(t))^2 - 4\det T(t) = 0, \qquad (\tr S(a))^2 - 4\det S(a) = 4-4(1+a^2) = -4a^2 < 0 .
\]
\end{theorem}

Hence translations are exactly parabolic and the velocity-addition matrices strictly elliptic. Since the discriminant is conjugation-invariant, the same classification is visible on the disc: $P(t)$ shears along circles tangent at $-1$, while $R(a)$ rotates rigidly about the centre.

\section{Conjugacy classification of real parabolics}\label{sec:classification}

\begin{theorem}[Every parabolic is a translation in disguise]\label{thm:conj}
Let $M$ be real with $\det M = 1$, $\tr M = 2$ and $M\neq I$. Then there exist a real $P$ with $\det P = 1$ and a real $s\neq0$ with $MP = P\,T(s)$, i.e.\ $M = P\,T(s)P^{-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $M = \begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}$, $a+d = 2$, $ad-bc = 1$.

\emph{Case $c = 0$.} Then $ad = 1$ and $a+d = 2$ force $a = d = 1$, so $M = T(b)$ with $b\neq0$ (else $M = I$); take $P = I$, $s = b$.

\emph{Case $c\neq0$.} Take $P = \begin{pmatrix}-(a-1)/c & 1\\ -1 & 0\end{pmatrix}$ and $s = -c$; then $\det P = 1$ and $s\neq0$. The entrywise verification of $MP = P\,T(s)$ uses only the relation $bc = -(a-1)^2$, which follows from $ad - bc = 1$ and $d = 2-a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cayley form of the classification]\label{thm:conjdisc}
Let $M$ be real with $\det M = 1$, $\tr M = 2$, $M\neq I$. Then there exist an invertible complex $X$ and a real $s\neq0$ with $MX = X\,P(s)$; one may take $X = PK^{-1}$ with $P,s$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:conj}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From Theorem~\ref{thm:intertwine}, $K^{-1}P(s) = T(s)K^{-1}$. Hence
\[
M(PK^{-1}) = (MP)K^{-1} = P\,T(s)K^{-1} = P\,K^{-1}P(s) = (PK^{-1})P(s),
\]
and $\det X = \det P/\det K = 1/(2i)\neq0$.
\end{proof}

Geometrically: after transfer to the disc, every non-identity parabolic becomes a horocyclic shear $P(s)$, hence by Theorem~\ref{thm:invariance} preserves \emph{some} horocycle foliation---just not, in general, the one at $\infty$. This closes the circle opened by Theorem~\ref{thm:counter}.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Horocycle-preservation test}
Given real $M$ with $\det M = 1$, Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity} yields an $O(1)$ test: check $c = 0$ and $|d| = 1$; return $(+,b)$ if $d=1$ and $(-,-b)$ if $d=-1$. Equivalently, numerically compare $\Imm(M\cdot i)$ with $1$ and $\Imm(M\cdot 2i)$ with $2$: two samples always suffice, which is exactly the content of the proof.

\subsection{Half-plane $\leftrightarrow$ disc transfer}
With $K^{-1} = \frac{1}{2i}\begin{psmallmatrix}1&-1\\ i& i\end{psmallmatrix}$, the transfer $M\mapsto KMK^{-1}$ costs two $2\times2$ complex multiplications. Theorem~\ref{thm:intertwine} gives $KT(t)K^{-1} = P(t)$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:ell} gives $KS(a)K^{-1} = R(a)$. The transfer preserves trace, determinant and hence conjugacy type.

\subsection{Parabolic normalisation}
Given real $M$ with $\det M = 1$, $\tr M = 2$, $M\neq I$: if $c = 0$ return $(I,b)$, else return $\big(\begin{psmallmatrix}-(a-1)/c&1\\-1&0\end{psmallmatrix},\,-c\big)$, in $O(1)$ arithmetic operations. Composing with the transfer gives the disc form of Theorem~\ref{thm:conjdisc}.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{What the rigidity theorem says.} $\SL$ is three-dimensional; the stabiliser of the cusp $\infty$ (upper-triangular matrices) is two-dimensional, containing translations and dilations $z\mapsto\lambda^2z$; requiring that \emph{each} horocycle be preserved cuts this to the one-dimensional unipotent subgroup, doubled by the centre $\{\pm I\}$, which acts trivially. The dilations are exactly what is lost.

\paragraph{Why the guard is unavoidable.} The trace condition is conjugation-invariant while horocycle preservation at $\infty$ is not; a conjugation-invariant condition can never characterise a non-invariant one, so some cusp-specific hypothesis is forced by pure logic. Theorem~\ref{thm:guarded} shows $M_{21}=0$ suffices and Theorem~\ref{thm:counter} shows something is needed.

\paragraph{The role of the intertwining identity.} Once Theorem~\ref{thm:intertwine} is available, the M\"obius-level statements follow from matrix algebra and the cocycle law. Identities between fractional-linear maps require case analysis on vanishing denominators; identities between matrices do not. The price is that matrices represent M\"obius maps only up to scalars, which is why the relation is stated as $KT(t) = P(t)K$ rather than with an implicit normalisation.

\paragraph{Connections.} The relation $T(1)\cdot z = z+1$ underlies the Fourier expansion of modular forms at the cusp, and Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity} characterises the unipotent part of the cusp stabiliser. The horocycle flow on quotients of $\SL$ is generated by $\{T(t)\}$, and the dictionary $h\circ C = \Imm$ transfers it to the bounded disc model without distortion. Theorem~\ref{thm:lin} identifies $\oplus$ with circle multiplication, the same phenomenon as additivity of rapidity in relativistic velocity composition. Theorem~\ref{thm:conj} supplies the normal form used to define cusp neighbourhoods and to run ping-pong arguments.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Ping-pong discreteness for the parabolic--elliptic pair.} For $\Gamma(t,a)\le\SL$ generated by $T(t)$ and $S(a)$, one expects an explicit real-algebraic threshold $\mathcal{T}(a)$ with $\Gamma(t,a)$ discrete and free of rank $2$ exactly when $|t|\ge\mathcal{T}(a)$, with $\mathcal{T}(a)$ a root of a polynomial in the entries of $R(a)$. After transfer to the disc, $h$ is a single scalar Lyapunov function---exactly invariant under $P(t)$, strictly monotone under $R(a)$ away from the fixed point---so ping-pong sets can be cut out by inequalities in $h$.
\item \textbf{Horocycle height as a group cocycle.} $\beta(g,z) = \log\big(h(g\cdot C(z))/h(C(z))\big)$ should be an $\RR$-valued cocycle on $\SL\times\HH$ whose vanishing locus is exactly $\{\pm T(t)\}$; Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity} then computes the kernel of a Busemann-type cocycle.
\item \textbf{All three species at once.} Characterise, for hyperbolic and elliptic elements, the exact families of foliations preserved, and identify the analogous guards.
\item \textbf{Quantitative horocycle displacement.} The ratio $\Imm(M\cdot z)/\Imm z = |cz+d|^{-2}$ is an explicit displacement function; uniform bounds over horocycle segments would yield effective equidistribution statements.
\item \textbf{Higher rank.} Extend the intertwining identity and the function $h$ to Siegel upper half-spaces and bounded symmetric domains.
\end{enumerate}

